---
The target function is `cJSON_AddObjectToObject`. Creates a new empty JSON object and adds it to `object` under `name`. Use it when you want to build nested objects. On success it returns the new child object; on failure it deletes it and returns `NULL`. Its signature is as follows:

```c

CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddObjectToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name);
```

The list of candidate functions from the library is:

1. `cJSON_AddItemToArray`
    `cJSON_AddItemToArray` appends an existing `cJSON` item to an array. Use it with a valid array node and a non-NULL item. It returns `cJSON_bool` success/failure; it’s a thin wrapper over `add_item_to_array`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Append item to the specified array/object. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_AddItemToArray(cJSON *array, cJSON *item);
    ```
2. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray`
    `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` removes the item at index `which` from a JSON array and frees it. Use it on a `cJSON` array pointer; if the index is invalid, `cJSON_DetachItemFromArray` returns `NULL`, and `cJSON_Delete(NULL)` is a no-op. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray(cJSON *array, int which);
    ```
3. `cJSON_CreateBool`
    Creates a new cJSON boolean item. Pass a `cJSON_bool`; nonzero becomes `cJSON_True`, zero becomes `cJSON_False`. Returns a newly allocated `cJSON*`, or `NULL` if allocation fails. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateBool(cJSON_bool boolean);
    ```
4. `cJSON_ParseWithLength`
    `cJSON_ParseWithLength` parses JSON from a non-NUL-terminated buffer of known length. Use it when input may contain embedded NULs or isn’t a C string. It just calls `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts(value, buffer_length, 0, 0)`; on failure it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_ParseWithLength(const char *value, size_t buffer_length);
    ```
5. `cJSON_CreateTrue`
    `cJSON_CreateTrue()` creates a new cJSON item of boolean true. Use it when building JSON trees programmatically. It allocates a node with `cJSON_New_Item(&global_hooks)`; on success sets `type = cJSON_True`, otherwise returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateTrue(void);
    ```
6. `cJSON_IsInvalid`
    `cJSON_IsInvalid` is a type-check helper. Use it to test whether a `cJSON*` item is marked `cJSON_Invalid`. It returns `false` for `NULL`, otherwise `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_Invalid`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* These functions check the type of an item */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsInvalid(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
7. `cJSON_Delete`
    `cJSON_Delete` frees a cJSON item and its owned subitems recursively. Use it when you’re done with a parsed/created tree. It walks `next` siblings, frees `child`, `valuestring`, and `string` unless marked `cJSON_IsReference`/`cJSON_StringIsConst`, then frees the item itself. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Delete a cJSON entity and all subentities. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void) cJSON_Delete(cJSON *item);
    ```
8. `cJSON_AddArrayToObject`
    `cJSON_AddArrayToObject` creates a new empty JSON array and adds it to `object` under `name`. Use it when you want to attach an array member, then fill that returned array. If insertion fails, it deletes the array and returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddArrayToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name);
    ```
9. `cJSON_Parse`
    `cJSON_Parse` is a convenience wrapper for `cJSON_ParseWithOpts(value, 0, 0)`. Use it to parse a JSON string into a `cJSON*` tree. On success, free with `cJSON_Delete`; on invalid JSON or parse failure, it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Memory Management: the caller is always responsible to free the results from all variants of cJSON_Parse (with cJSON_Delete) and cJSON_Print (with stdlib free, cJSON_Hooks.free_fn, or cJSON_free as appropriate). The exception is cJSON_PrintPreallocated, where the caller has full responsibility of the buffer. */
/* Supply a block of JSON, and this returns a cJSON object you can interrogate. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_Parse(const char *value);
    ```
10. `cJSON_GetNumberValue`
    `cJSON_GetNumberValue` returns a `double` from a cJSON item only if it is a number. Use it after checking/expecting a numeric item. If `item` is not a number, it returns `NAN`; otherwise it returns `item->valuedouble`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(double) cJSON_GetNumberValue(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
11. `cJSON_SetNumberHelper`
    Helper for `cJSON_SetNumberValue`: set a cJSON number’s `valuedouble` and clamped `valueint`. Use only on a valid `cJSON*`. If `object==NULL`, it returns `NAN`. `valueint` is clamped to `INT_MIN/INT_MAX`; otherwise it’s `(int)number`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* helper for the cJSON_SetNumberValue macro */
CJSON_PUBLIC(double) cJSON_SetNumberHelper(cJSON *object, double number);
    ```
12. `cJSON_ReplaceItemInObjectCaseSensitive`
    Replaces an object member by key using case-sensitive matching. Use with a cJSON object, key string, and new item. Returns true on success; false if object/key/item is invalid or the key isn’t found. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_ReplaceItemInObjectCaseSensitive(cJSON *object,const char *string,cJSON *newitem);
    ```
13. `cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive`
    `cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive` looks up a named child in a JSON object using **case-sensitive** key matching. Use it with an object node and a C string key. It returns the matching `cJSON*`, or `NULL` if `object`/`string` is invalid or no exact-case key exists. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive(const cJSON * const object, const char * const string);
    ```
14. `cJSON_CreateIntArray`
    Creates a cJSON array from `count` ints in `numbers`. Use with a valid non-NULL array and `count >= 0`; `count` must not exceed the source length. Returns NULL on invalid args or allocation failure; otherwise a new array of number items. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* These utilities create an Array of count items.
 * The parameter count cannot be greater than the number of elements in the number array, otherwise array access will be out of bounds.*/
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateIntArray(const int *numbers, int count);
    ```
15. `cJSON_AddNullToObject`
    Creates a JSON `null` item and adds it to an object under `name` in one step. Use with a valid object and key. Returns the added item on success, `NULL` on failure; if add fails, the created item is deleted. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Helper functions for creating and adding items to an object at the same time.
