CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON*) cJSON_AddNullToObject(cJSON * const object, const char * const name);
    ```
16. `cJSON_CreateNull`
    `cJSON_CreateNull()` allocates a new `cJSON` item and marks it as `cJSON_NULL`. Use it when building a JSON tree and you need a JSON `null` value. If allocation fails, it returns `NULL`; otherwise it returns the new item. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* These calls create a cJSON item of the appropriate type. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateNull(void);
    ```
17. `cJSON_CreateObject`
    `cJSON_CreateObject()` allocates a new empty `cJSON` item and marks it as an object (`cJSON_Object`). Use it as the root or a container, then add members with cJSON add-item APIs. If allocation fails, it returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_CreateObject(void);
    ```
18. `cJSON_IsNull`
    `cJSON_IsNull` checks whether a `cJSON` item is JSON `null`. Use it on a `cJSON*` node. It returns `false` if `item == NULL`; otherwise `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsNull(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
19. `cJSON_malloc`
    `cJSON_malloc` is a thin wrapper around the allocator set by `cJSON_InitHooks`. Use it when you need cJSON’s configured heap behavior. It simply calls `global_hooks.allocate(size)` and returns that pointer; behavior depends entirely on the installed hook. Its signature is:
    ```c
