user:
---
The target function is `cJSON_IsNull`. `cJSON_IsNull` checks whether a `cJSON` item is JSON `null`. Use it on a `cJSON*` node. It returns `false` if `item == NULL`; otherwise `true` only when `(item->type & 0xFF) == cJSON_NULL`. Its signature is as follows:

```c

CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsNull(const cJSON * const item);
```

The list of candidate functions from the library is:

1. `cJSON_malloc`
    `cJSON_malloc` is a thin wrapper around the allocator set by `cJSON_InitHooks`. Use it when you need cJSON’s configured heap behavior. It simply calls `global_hooks.allocate(size)` and returns that pointer; behavior depends entirely on the installed hook. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* malloc/free objects using the malloc/free functions that have been set with cJSON_InitHooks */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void *) cJSON_malloc(size_t size);
    ```
2. `cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts`
    Parses JSON from a memory buffer of known length. Use `value` + `buffer_length`; optionally get end position via `return_parse_end`. Fails on NULL/zero length or parse errors. `require_null_terminated` controls whether trailing non-NUL data is allowed. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_ParseWithLengthOpts(const char *value, size_t buffer_length, const char **return_parse_end, cJSON_bool require_null_terminated);
    ```
3. `cJSON_GetArrayItem`
    `cJSON_GetArrayItem` returns the item at a 0-based index from a cJSON array. Use it with a nonnegative index; negative values return `NULL`. If the array is `NULL` or the index is out of range, it also returns `NULL`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Retrieve item number "index" from array "array". Returns NULL if unsuccessful. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON *) cJSON_GetArrayItem(const cJSON *array, int index);
    ```
4. `cJSON_Version`
    `cJSON_Version()` returns a static, NUL-terminated string with the library version (`major.minor.patch`). Use it read-only; don’t free or modify it. Each call rewrites the same buffer and returns the same pointer. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* returns the version of cJSON as a string */
CJSON_PUBLIC(const char*) cJSON_Version(void);
    ```
5. `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS`
    `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` adds `item` to `object` using a const key string. It marks the key as constant, so cJSON won’t free it. Use only for literals/long-lived strings. Returns `false` on add failure; otherwise `true`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Use this when string is definitely const (i.e. a literal, or as good as), and will definitely survive the cJSON object.
 * WARNING: When this function was used, make sure to always check that (item->type & cJSON_StringIsConst) is zero before
 * writing to `item->string` */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
    ```
6. `cJSON_IsString`
    `cJSON_IsString` checks whether a `cJSON` item is a string. Use it to safely test an item before treating it as a string. It returns `false` for `NULL`, and `true` only when the item’s type is `cJSON_String` (low 8 bits). Its signature is:
    ```c
    
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_IsString(const cJSON * const item);
    ```
7. `cJSON_PrintPreallocated`
    `cJSON_PrintPreallocated` renders a `cJSON` item into a caller-provided buffer, avoiding internal allocation. Use it with a valid buffer and length; it returns 1 on success, 0 on failure. It fails for `NULL` buffer or negative length, and may need ~5 extra bytes for safety. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Render a cJSON entity to text using a buffer already allocated in memory with given length. Returns 1 on success and 0 on failure. */
/* NOTE: cJSON is not always 100% accurate in estimating how much memory it will use, so to be safe allocate 5 bytes more than you actually need */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_PrintPreallocated(cJSON *item, char *buffer, const int length, const cJSON_bool format);
    ```
8. `cJSON_ReplaceItemViaPointer`
    Replaces `item` in `parent` with `replacement` by pointer, preserving list links and shifting later items right. Use only with valid `parent/item/replacement`; returns false on NULLs, true if `replacement==item`. Its signature is:
    ```c
    /* Shifts pre-existing items to the right. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool) cJSON_ReplaceItemViaPointer(cJSON * const parent, cJSON * const item, cJSON * replacement);
    ```
9. `cJSON_HasObjectItem`
    `cJSON_HasObjectItem` is a convenience check for object members. Pass a cJSON object and key string; it returns `1` if `cJSON_GetObjectItem(object, string)` finds a match, otherwise `0`. It’s just a presence test, not a value fetch. Its signature is:
    ```c
    
