## Target API Functions

You should invoke the functions in the following order: `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive` -> `cJSON_Minify` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete` -> `cJSON_Delete`

Below is the detailed information:

1. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray`
​ `cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray` removes the item at index `which` from a JSON array and frees it. Use it on a `cJSON` array pointer; if the index is invalid, `cJSON_DetachItemFromArray` returns `NULL`, and `cJSON_Delete(NULL)` is a no-op.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromArray(cJSON *array, int which);
​ ```
2. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive`
​ Deletes a case-sensitive object member by key. Use it on a cJSON object and a string key; it detaches the matching item, then frees it with `cJSON_Delete`. If no match is found, nothing is deleted.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromObjectCaseSensitive(cJSON *object, const char *string);
​ ```
3. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray`
​ Adds a reference, not a copy, of `item` to `array`. Use it when reusing an existing cJSON node in another array without altering the original. Returns false if `array` is NULL; otherwise it wraps `item` via `create_reference()` and appends it.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Append reference to item to the specified array/object. Use this when you want to add an existing cJSON to a new cJSON, but don't want to corrupt your existing cJSON. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemReferenceToArray(cJSON *array, cJSON *item);
​ ```
4. `cJSON_Delete`
​ `cJSON_Delete` frees a cJSON item and its owned subitems recursively. Use it when you’re done with a parsed/created tree. It walks `next` siblings, frees `child`, `valuestring`, and `string` unless marked `cJSON_IsReference`/`cJSON_StringIsConst`, then frees the item itself.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Delete a cJSON entity and all subentities. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_Delete(cJSON *item);
​ ```
5. `cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject`
​ Deletes the first object member with the given key. Use it on a `cJSON` object and key string; it detaches the matching item and frees it via `cJSON_Delete`. If no match exists, nothing is deleted.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_DeleteItemFromObject(cJSON *object, const char *string);
​ ```
6. `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS`
​ `cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS` adds `item` to `object` using a const key string. It marks the key as constant, so cJSON won’t free it. Use only for literals/long-lived strings. Returns `false` on add failure; otherwise `true`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Use this when string is definitely const (i.e. a literal, or as good as), and will definitely survive the cJSON object.
 * WARNING: When this function was used, make sure to always check that (item->type & cJSON_StringIsConst) is zero before
 * writing to `item->string` */
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemToObjectCS(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
7. `cJSON_Minify`
​ `cJSON_Minify` is an in-place JSON cleaner: it removes whitespace and C-style comments from a writable `char *`. Pass a mutable buffer, not a string literal. If `json == NULL`, it returns. It scans and compacts the text into the same buffer.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* Minify a strings, remove blank characters(such as ' ', '\t', '\r', '\n') from strings.
 * The input pointer json cannot point to a read-only address area, such as a string constant,
 * but should point to a readable and writable address area. */
CJSON_PUBLIC(void)
cJSON_Minify(char *json);
​ ```
8. `cJSON_AddItemToObject`
​ `cJSON_AddItemToObject` adds an existing `cJSON` item into an object under the given key. Use it with a valid object, key string, and item. It returns `cJSON_bool` from the internal add helper; behavior depends on that helper’s success/failure.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```
9. `cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject`
​ Adds a key/value to a JSON object as a **reference** to `item`, not a deep copy. Use it when the same cJSON node should appear in multiple places. Returns false if `object` or `string` is NULL; success depends on `add_item_to_object`/reference creation.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
CJSON_PUBLIC(cJSON_bool)
cJSON_AddItemReferenceToObject(cJSON *object, const char *string, cJSON *item);
​ ```

## Related Data Types

```c
/* The cJSON structure: */
struct cJSON {
 /* next/prev allow you to walk array/object chains. Alternatively, use GetArraySize/GetArrayItem/GetObjectItem */
 struct cJSON *next;
 struct cJSON *prev;
 /* An array or object item will have a child pointer pointing to a chain of the items in the array/object. */
 struct cJSON *child;

 /* The type of the item, as above. */
 int type;

 /* The item's string, if type==cJSON_String and type == cJSON_Raw */
 char *valuestring;
 /* writing to valueint is DEPRECATED, use cJSON_SetNumberValue instead */
 int valueint;
 /* The item's number, if type==cJSON_Number */
 double valuedouble;

 /* The item's name string, if this item is the child of, or is in the list of subitems of an object. */
 char *string;
} cJSON;

typedef int cJSON_bool;
/* The cJSON structure: */
typedef struct cJSON cJSON;
